        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Caodaism</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">1925 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Origin</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a>, Southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, in 1925, as a Spiritualist club; officially established in the city of Tây Ninh in 1926.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Founder or early teachers</span><span class="info-stat-val">Cao Quỳnh Cư, Cao Hoài Sang, Lê Văn Trung and Phạm Công Tắc, clerks in the colonial government who founded the Spiritualist club in 1925; Ngô Văn Chiêu, revered as the first disciple of the Đức Cao Đài, signed the declaration of founding on 7 October 1926.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Scriptures</span><span class="info-stat-val">Several sets of scriptures; those of the Tây Ninh Holy See include Kinh Thiên Đạo Và Thế Đạo (Prayers of the Heavenly and the Earthly Way), Pháp Chánh Truyền (the Religious Constitution), Tân Luật (the Canonical Codes) and Con Đường Thiêng Liêng Hằng Sống (Divine Path to Eternal Life).</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Followers today</span><span class="info-stat-val">Estimates vary: government figures give 4.4 million affiliated to the Tây Ninh Holy See and up to 6 million with other branches; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> estimated about 2.5 million in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> in January 2015; tens of thousands live abroad, mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>.</span></div>
