    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Caodaists worship one God, Cao Đài, and hold that all religions come from the same source; they believe that God has revealed his will in three eras and that the third and final revelation is given through the Cao Đài faith.</li><li>The religion began in 1925 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> with a group of colonial clerks who held séances, and was officially established at Tây Ninh in 1926; by 1940 it had gathered over a million members.</li><li>Its church is organised in a hierarchy with a Pope, Cardinals, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishops</a> and Priests, and its clergy wear yellow, azure or red for the Buddhist, Taoist and Confucianist branches.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Caodaists hold that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-hugo">Victor Hugo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a> and Trạng Trình, the Vietnamese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nostradamus">Nostradamus</a>, are the three main prophets of the Cao Đài pantheon, as a plaque at the entrance of the Tây Ninh Temple shows.</p></aside>
    </section>

