      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1925</span><span class="rf-text">Cao Quỳnh Cư, Cao Hoài Sang, Lê Văn Trung and Phạm Công Tắc found a Spiritualist club named AĂÂ in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> to practise mediumship</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1926</span><span class="rf-text">Ngô Văn Chiêu signs the Declaration of the Founding of the Cao Đài Religion on 7 October, and the religion is officially established at Tây Ninh</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1934</span><span class="rf-text">Lê Văn Trung, the first and only officially ordained Pope of the Tây Ninh Holy See, dies and Phạm Công Tắc takes over his role</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1940</span><span class="rf-text">The religion has gathered over a million members, between a fifth and a quarter of the population of Cochinchina</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1941</span><span class="rf-text">Phạm Công Tắc and other Caodaist leaders are arrested and jailed in French penal colonies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>, where they stay until 1946</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1975</span><span class="rf-text">After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> the incoming communist government bans the practice of Caodaism</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1997</span><span class="rf-text">Caodaism is granted legal recognition and unrestricted practice once again</span></li></ol>
